        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.095,
            0.538,
            0.113
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.249,
            0.132,
            0.865,
            0.218
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In 2010, scientist J. Craig Venter and his team made an important discovery in the field of synthetic biology. They first studied the complete DNA sequence of a simple bacterium called Mycoplasma mycoides using a computer programming. Then, they chemically synthesised (in the laboratory) an exact copy of this DNA."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.249,
            0.22,
            0.864,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Next, they took another closely related bacterium and removed its DNA, but kept the rest of the cell (such as the cytoplasm and the cell membrane) intact. They inserted the synthetic DNA into this cell. After this, the cell started to grow and divide, following the instructions from the newly inserted synthetic DNA. This experiment showed that DNA controls the structure and activities of a cell."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.325,
            0.864,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "However, scientists did not create a completely new cell from scratch. Only the DNA was synthetic. The other parts of the cell were taken from an already existing living cell."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.387,
            0.192,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.076,
                0.387,
                0.192,
                0.583
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.058,
                0.591,
                0.214,
                0.634
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 2.16: Growing roots of an onion in a jar containing water"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.406,
            0.795,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.4 How do Normal Cells Grow and Divide?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.431,
            0.89,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When you get a small cut on your skin, it heals after a few days. When hair fall out, new hair grow back. How does this happen? It happens because "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cells in our body can grow and divide",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " to replace the old, dead, or damaged cells. When our body grows, it is not just because cells get bigger. Cells can grow only up to a certain size but growth happens because cells divide to form new cells. Let us study freshly growing root tips of an onion to understand how cells divide (Fig. 2.16)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.561,
            0.643,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 2.5: Let us enhance our skills"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.583,
            0.639,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Take a jar and fill it up with plain water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.603,
            0.888,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Now, place an onion bulb over the jar in such a way that its base bearing roots, is immersed in the water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.64,
            0.889,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Leave the setup for 5–6 days and observe. Do you observe the roots growing? Cut 2–3 cm of the freshly grown roots and transfer them to freshly prepared aceto-alcohol (glacial acetic acid:ethanol :: 1:3). Keep the root tips in aceto-alcohol for 24 hours and then transfer them to 70 per cent ethanol (for preservation)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.729,
            0.888,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Take one or two preserved roots, wash them in water and then, place them on a clean slide."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.766,
            0.889,
            0.818
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Put one drop of dilute Hydrochloric acid (HCl) on the root tips to soften the tissue. Rinse the roots after 10–15 minutes. Then add 2–3 drops of aceto-carmine stain on them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.82,
            0.888,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Leave the slide for 5–10 minutes and then, gently warm it (with caution) over a spirit lamp."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.858,
            0.889,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Cut the tip portion of the root on the slide and put a coverslip. Gently squash the coverslip with your thumb to spread the cells on the slide."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.912,
            0.619,
            0.931
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Observe the slide under a microscope."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
